From bec1baf9c5871158647d7080536037960041dfc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 09:11:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] fix(operator): a Safe's guard and fallback handler are
 authority too -- read at boot and before the handover

The "no authority" check and `jinn signer-migrate` read a Safe's owners and modules, not its
guard or its fallback handler. A guard runs before and after every transaction of the Safe and
can veto each; the fallback handler answers what the Safe does not implement, `isValidSignature`
among it, so it decides the Safe's ERC-1271 answers. Safe 1.3.0 has no getter for either and keeps
them at fixed storage slots. A point the fixes of findings AK-2026-11-28-02 and -03 (the internal
audit of 2026-11-28) left open.

- signer/attestation.ts: readSafeGuardAndHandler reads both slots (`eth_getStorageAt`:
  keccak256("guard_manager.guard.address") and keccak256("fallback_manager.handler.address"))
  and says what in them is refused:
  - a guard other than zero, unless it is also an enabled module of the Safe. A module already
    runs any call from the Safe, `setGuard` included, so such a guard is the module the operator
    reviews. stOLAS sets its MultisigGuard so on every service Safe it creates, and that guard
    keeps itself set (it reverts every transaction after which the Safe's guard is another):
    refusing it would refuse every staked operator, with no way out;
  - a fallback handler other than zero or Safe's CompatibilityFallbackHandler of the Safe's
    `VERSION()`. For 1.3.0 safe-global/safe-deployments records two deployments of it on Base and
    Base Sepolia, both accepted: canonical 0xf48f2B2d2a534e402487b3ee7C18c33Aec0Fe5e4 (stOLAS's
    deploy script sets it on the service Safe) and eip155
    0x017062a1dE2FE6b99BE3d9d37841FeD19F573804 (protocol-kit takes it for the fleet identity Safe
    on Base). A handler of a version without a record is refused.
  assertAgentEoaHasNoAuthority refuses on either, saying what it found, the call that clears it
  and that the owner clears it with a root operation (a transaction of the Safe from the Kernel,
  signed with the owner key on the root path; the agent's mandate refuses the Safe's own
  administration). It resolves with the Safe's guard as well.
- signer/migrate.ts: the same reads in the first step, before anything changes hands and in a
  dry run too: refused, nothing is changed. A guard that is one of the modules is named as the
  guard in `safe-modules`' review.
- main.ts: the boot warning about a module says when it is the Safe's guard as well.
- tests: unit (a guard that is not a module refused; one that is passes and is named; no handler
  and both deployments of the 1.3.0 handler pass; any other handler refused, and one of a version
  without a record, "constructor" included; every refusal named at once; the migration refuses in
  a dry run too and sends nothing). On Safe 1.3.0's own bytecode,
  test/hermetic/signer-migrate-refusals.test.ts: a guard set with setGuard is read from its slot
  and refused, by the migration and, after a swap by hand, by the check; as a module it passes
  and is named; Safes set up with either 1.3.0 handler pass, one with another handler is refused.
  test/hermetic/full-loop-kernel-owner.test.ts, on the operator's own Safes: the staked service
  Safe's guard is one of its modules and its handler the canonical one, and the migration names
  the guard; the fleet identity Safe has no guard and the eip155 handler, and while the EOA still
  owns it (the loop hands over the service Safe only) the check refuses it by name.
  test/e2e/_daemon-harness-helpers.ts: bootstrapStakedOperator returns the fleet identity Safe.
  Each rule was broken once on purpose and a test caught it (no guard refused, every guard
  refused, any handler accepted, the two slots swapped, no pre-check in the migration, the check
  ignoring both).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
